io_client`/`ClientSession` contexts, because exiting them from another task fails with "Attempted to exit cancel scope in a different task" (measured, fact 3.4c). A server that fails to start is marked `down` and never stops the others, and `start()` waits at most `RESTART_READY_TIMEOUT_S` (30 s) — a server still starting then is stopped and marked `down` ("start timed out"), because `get_shared_registry` holds its lock across `start()`; a dead child — which otherwise makes `call_tool` raise `ClosedResourceError` forever (fact 3.4a) — is restarted by the failing call and the call retried once, with a backoff of 3 restarts per 60 s; a down server's tools answer `"<tool> failed: <server> is unavailable (...); it restarts automatically."`, never "Unknown tool". A down server's tools are on no menu, so no call would name them: `revive_down()` background-restarts every server that is not up (including one that failed the first `start()`), backoff permitting, on every menu read (`openai_tools()`/`tools_for()`) and every reuse of the shared registry. The owner task registers its own tools when it comes up, so a restart whose caller was cancelled still restores them. `stop()` sets `_closing` first, so a restart or revive still in flight when it runs spawns nothing, and a cancelled `start()` stops what it spawned. `status()` feeds the `mcp_registry_status` log line after every restart; find restarts with `log_search(source="bot", query="mcp_server_")` — the sidecar cannot see the bot's registry. `requires_env` / `optional_env` together must name **every** environment variable the server reads (directly or through a `jarvis.*` import) that is not in `jarvis/skills/registry.py`'s `BASE_ENV_KEYS`. They are an allowlist, not documentation: after `MORTIMER_SECURITY_HARDENING_PLAN.md` K2, an undeclared variable does not reach the child. Use `requires_env` for a variable with no code default (`check_skills.py` fails if it is unset) and `optional_env` for one that has a default (forwarded if present, never required). `requires_env_dynamic` is for a variable whose *name* is chosen at runtime (only `mcp-screen` today). `mcp_repo` (`repo_read_file`/`repo_list_files`/`repo_search`/`repo_write_file`/`repo_commit_write`) is deliberately a separate server from `mcp_apps`: `mcp_apps` is GitHub-only (only `mcp_apps/github.py` touches the network); `mcp_repo` is the local working tree on this machine, path-confined by `resolve_repo_path` (deny-lists for both readable and, separately, writable paths — `.git`/`.env`/secrets/config/CI/lockfiles) and gated by the same draft→confirm `actions`-table pattern `mcp_git` uses for commits. Every tool description on both servers states which one it is ("Local working tree on this machine:" vs "GitHub:") because tool selection happens off the schema text, not off `config/agents.yaml` alone (`docs/plans/implemented/MORTIMER_AGENT_TRUST_PLAN.md` D13/D14).
 
